204 — Persistent Volumes and Storage
Master persistent storage in Kubernetes using Persistent Volumes, Persistent Volume Claims, and Storage Classes for stateful applications.

Step 1 Create a Persistent Volume
Define storage resources available in the cluster.

Commands to Run
cat > persistent-volume.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: my-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 1Gi
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  hostPath:
    path: /data/pv
EOF

kubectl apply -f persistent-volume.yaml
kubectl get pv

What This Does
Persistent Volumes are cluster resources representing physical storage. Administrators provision PVs, users claim them with PVCs.

Expected Outcome
PV created with 1Gi capacity, STATUS shows Available, ACCESS MODES shows RWO.

Pro Tips
1. PVs are cluster-wide resources (not namespaced)
2. AccessModes: ReadWriteOnce (RWO), ReadOnlyMany (ROX), ReadWriteMany (RWX)
3. hostPath is for testing only - use cloud storage in production
4. Status: Available → Bound → Released
5. Reclaim policy: Retain, Delete, or Recycle

Step 2 Create a Persistent Volume Claim
Request storage for your application.

Commands to Run
cat > persistent-volume-claim.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: my-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 500Mi
EOF

kubectl apply -f persistent-volume-claim.yaml
kubectl get pvc
kubectl get pv

What This Does
PVCs are requests for storage. Kubernetes binds PVCs to suitable PVs based on size and access modes.

Expected Outcome
PVC created and STATUS shows Bound. PV status changes from Available to Bound.

Pro Tips
1. PVCs are namespaced, PVs are not
2. PVC requests minimum storage size
3. Bound PVC is matched to a PV
4. Binding considers: access mode, size, storage class, selectors
5. One PV can only bind to one PVC

Step 3 Use PVC in a Pod
Mount persistent storage in a container.

Commands to Run
cat > pod-with-pvc.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: storage-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: alpine:latest
    command: ["sh", "-c", "echo 'Hello from persistent storage' > /data/message.txt && sleep 3600"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: storage
      mountPath: /data
  volumes:
  - name: storage
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: my-pvc
EOF

kubectl apply -f pod-with-pvc.yaml
kubectl exec storage-pod -- cat /data/message.txt

What This Does
Pods reference PVCs in volumes section. Data written to mountPath persists beyond pod lifetime.

Expected Outcome
Pod created. File written to /data persists on the PV.

Pro Tips
1. Multiple pods can use same PVC if access mode allows
2. Data persists even if pod is deleted
3. Pod and PVC must be in same namespace
4. mountPath is where volume appears in container
5. Data survives pod restarts and recreation

Step 4 Test data persistence
Verify data survives pod deletion and recreation.

Commands to Run
kubectl delete pod storage-pod
kubectl apply -f pod-with-pvc.yaml
kubectl exec storage-pod -- cat /data/message.txt

What This Does
Delete and recreate pod. Data persists because it's stored on the PV, not in the container.

Expected Outcome
File still exists with same content after pod recreation. Data persisted!

Pro Tips
1. This is the key benefit of persistent volumes
2. Essential for databases, file uploads, logs
3. Container storage is ephemeral, PV storage persists
4. Backup PV data regularly in production
5. Consider using VolumeSnapshots for backups

Step 5 Create a Storage Class
Enable dynamic provisioning of volumes.

Commands to Run
kubectl get storageclass
Copy
cat > storageclass.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-storage
provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
EOF

kubectl apply -f storageclass.yaml
kubectl get sc

What This Does
Storage Classes enable dynamic volume provisioning. Cloud providers offer SSD, HDD, and other storage types.

Expected Outcome
Storage class created. Default storage class shown with (default) annotation.

Pro Tips
1. Storage Classes abstract storage details
2. Cloud providers auto-create storage classes
3. provisioner varies by platform (AWS: ebs, GCP: pd, Azure: disk)
4. volumeBindingMode: Immediate or WaitForFirstConsumer
5. Use for SSD vs HDD, different IOPS, backup policies
6. Mark as default with annotation: storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"

Step 6 Use dynamic provisioning
Create PVC that automatically provisions a PV.

Commands to Run
cat > dynamic-pvc.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: dynamic-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: standard
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi
EOF

kubectl apply -f dynamic-pvc.yaml
kubectl get pvc dynamic-pvc
kubectl get pv

What This Does
Specifying storageClassName triggers dynamic provisioning. Storage Class provisioner creates PV automatically.

Expected Outcome
PVC created. On cloud providers, PV auto-created. On minikube, may stay Pending.

Pro Tips
1. No need to manually create PVs with dynamic provisioning
2. Default storage class used if not specified
3. On minikube, standard class uses hostPath
4. Cloud providers create real disks (EBS, PD, etc.)
5. Most production clusters use dynamic provisioning
6. Deleting PVC may delete PV (depends on reclaim policy)

Step 7 Use volumes in StatefulSets
Create persistent storage for each pod in a StatefulSet.

Commands to Run
cat > statefulset-storage.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: web
spec:
  serviceName: web
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:alpine
        volumeMounts:
        - name: data
          mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html
  volumeClaimTemplates:
  - metadata:
      name: data
    spec:
      accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 100Mi
EOF

kubectl apply -f statefulset-storage.yaml
kubectl get pvc

What This Does
StatefulSets use volumeClaimTemplates to create unique PVC for each pod. Each pod gets its own persistent storage.

Expected Outcome
2 PVCs created: data-web-0 and data-web-1. Each pod has dedicated storage.

Pro Tips
1. StatefulSets create one PVC per replica
2. PVC names include pod ordinal: [name]-[statefulset]-[ordinal]
3. Essential for databases and stateful apps
4. Scaling up creates new PVCs
5. Scaling down doesn't delete PVCs (manual cleanup needed)
6. Each pod maintains its storage across restarts

Step 8 Resize a Persistent Volume Claim
Expand storage capacity of existing PVC.

Commands to Run
kubectl patch pvc my-pvc -p '{"spec":{"resources":{"requests":{"storage":"2Gi"}}}}'
kubectl get pvc my-pvc

What This Does
Some storage classes support volume expansion. Update PVC size to expand underlying volume.

Expected Outcome
PVC size updated. May require pod restart for filesystem resize.

Pro Tips
1. Storage class must allow volume expansion (allowVolumeExpansion: true)
2. Can only increase size, not decrease
3. Some volumes require pod restart to resize filesystem
4. Check storage class: kubectl get sc [name] -o yaml
5. Not all provisioners support expansion
6. Cloud providers usually support this feature

Step 9 View volume details and status
Inspect volume configuration and bindings.

Commands to Run
kubectl describe pv
kubectl describe pvc my-pvc
kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.kind=PersistentVolumeClaim

What This Does
Detailed info shows capacity, access modes, reclaim policy, and mount status.

Expected Outcome
Full details of PVs and PVCs including status, capacity, and events.

Pro Tips
1. Check Status, Capacity, Access Modes, Reclaim Policy
2. Events show provisioning, binding, and mount issues
3. Use when troubleshooting storage problems
4. Claim Ref shows which PVC bound to which PV
5. StorageClass shows which class was used

Step 10 Clean up storage resources
Delete PVCs and understand reclaim policies.

Commands to Run
kubectl delete pod storage-pod
kubectl delete pvc my-pvc dynamic-pvc
kubectl get pv
kubectl delete statefulset web
kubectl delete pvc data-web-0 data-web-1
kubectl delete pv my-pv
rm -f *.yaml

What This Does
Deleting PVC may delete or retain PV based on reclaim policy (Delete, Retain, Recycle).

Expected Outcome
PVCs deleted. PVs status depends on reclaim policy. Manual PV requires explicit deletion.

Pro Tips
1. Reclaim policies: Delete (auto-delete PV), Retain (keep PV)
2. Check policy before deleting production PVCs
3. StatefulSet PVCs aren't auto-deleted with StatefulSet
4. Always backup data before deleting storage
5. Released PVs can't be rebound without manual cleanup
6. Consider using VolumeSnapshots before deletion

